# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

Creates an Access Control List (ACL) in a Redpanda cluster.

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/docs/acl/main.tf" }}

### Existing cluster

ACLs do not need to live in the same configuration as the cluster. Point `cluster_api_url` at an existing cluster's data plane URL and iterate over the operations each principal needs. Every operation is a separate ACL, so `for_each` over an operation list keeps a permission set readable as it grows.

{{ tffile "examples/docs/acl_existing_cluster/main.tf" }}

Kafka defines which operations apply to each resource type. A `TOPIC` accepts `READ`, `WRITE`, `CREATE`, `DELETE`, `ALTER`, `DESCRIBE`, `ALTER_CONFIGS` and `DESCRIBE_CONFIGS`; a `GROUP` accepts `READ`, `DESCRIBE` and `DELETE`; a `CLUSTER` accepts `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DESCRIBE`, `CLUSTER_ACTION`, `ALTER_CONFIGS`, `DESCRIBE_CONFIGS` and `IDEMPOTENT_WRITE`. The broker stores any combination, so an ACL such as `READ` on `CLUSTER` applies without error and grants nothing.

## Limitations

We are not currently able to support ACL creation in self hosted clusters. This is an area of active development so expect that to change soon.

## Import

We do not support the import of ACLs into the Redpanda provider at this time.

